[2026-09-30 02:00:54] [MODULE] Customer Profile: photo survives an update + camera icon — Fixed a real bug found by driving the page in a headless browser: saving your profile silently deleted your profile picture. Root cause was the remove control, not the save. It was a permanent <input type="hidden" name="remove_photo" value="1"> sitting in the form, plus a button whose handler ran $el.closest('form').querySelector('[name=remove_photo]').checked = true — a no-op, because a hidden input has no checked state. So the flag went out with EVERY save, and ProfileController::update() takes its 'elseif ($request->boolean("remove_photo"))' branch whenever no new file was chosen: it deleted the file and nulled the column. Editing a name wiped your photo. Reproduced it live — a browser submit of the form deleted the dev user's photo and left profile-photos/ empty. This predates the card redesign; the redesign just carried the markup forward. Fix in resources/views/customer/profile/edit.blade.php: the hidden input and the JS button are gone, replaced by a single <button type="submit" name="remove_photo" value="1">. A submit button only contributes its name and value when it is the button that submitted the form, which is exactly the semantics wanted, and it removes the JavaScript dependency. Also swapped the camera badge's hand-written tray/photo SVG for <x-heroicon-o-camera />, which is what the user asked for and matches the heroicons the rest of the card uses; the badge remains the file input's <label for="profile_photo"> so clicking it opens the picker and the input stays keyboard- and screen-reader reachable. Added 3 tests: a name-only save keeps the photo (both the DB path and the stored file), the remove control still deletes both, and no remove flag sits idle in the form while a submit button carries it. All three failed before the fix and pass after. Verified in headless Chrome: badge SVG is now the camera path; remove button is type=submit name=remove_photo value=1 with the hidden input gone; a name-only save shows 'Your profile has been updated.' and the photo is still on the page; clicking Remove current picture clears the photo, falls back to initials and removes the button; no exceptions. Restored the dev user's name and photo state and removed the seeded file. 339 tests pass.
