[2026-09-30 02:14:59] [MODULE] Customer Profile: avatar actually displays — Found why the profile picture looked like it was not saving. It WAS saving — the file landed on disk and the column was written — but the avatar never displayed, so from the page it looked like a failed save. Root cause: APP_URL is http://localhost:8000 while the project is browsed at http://localhost/BTA/public/ through XAMPP, and all four avatar call sites used Storage::url(), which builds from APP_URL. So the <img> requested http://localhost:8000/storage/... — a different origin, nothing listening — and the browser showed a broken image for a perfectly good file. I proved it with a real browser upload over CDP: DOM.setFileInputFiles attached a 234 KB JPEG, the form posted multipart/form-data, the response was 200, the toast said 'Your profile has been updated.', the <img> was rendered with profile_photo_path set — and image request 'loaded: false', src on port 8000 while the page was on 8136. Fix: App\Models\User::getProfilePhotoUrlAttribute now returns url('storage/'.) instead of asset('storage/'.). url() resolves against the current request's root, so it follows whatever host, port or subdirectory the browser actually asked for, and the accessor becomes the one source of truth. Rewired all four call sites to it: customer/profile/edit.blade.php, customer/dashboard.blade.php, admin/users/show.blade.php and partials/customer/nav.blade.php — no Storage::url() or hand-built storage URL is left in any view, so the nav and dashboard avatars are fixed too rather than only the profile page. Verified end to end in Chrome after the fix: the uploaded photo returns HTTP 200, the avatar is complete:true with naturalWidth 1530, and the nav avatar renders from the same URL. Two tests added: the avatar src resolves against the request and never contains APP_URL, and no view still builds a storage URL by hand. Also worth noting for the user: MCACAFE_v1's save path is identical to BTA's (hasFile -> delete old -> store on the public disk), so the reference was never the difference — their APP_URL presumably matches how they serve it. 341 tests pass. Also advised: setting APP_URL to whatever origin they actually browse from is still worth doing, since Laravel uses it for redirects and mail links, even though avatars no longer depend on it.
