[2026-10-02 04:28:07] [MODULE] Services photo fix + breadcrumb removal — Two changes on /services and /services/{slug}. (1) PHOTO NO LONGER RESIZES. Root cause: the card is a flex row, and a flex item is align-self:stretch by default, which sets the photo wrapper's height from the price list beside it and silently overrides aspect-ratio - the browser reported aspect-ratio 4/5 and then ignored it. Measured before: 160x292 collapsed, 160x562 expanded on the SAME card, and 292 vs 206 between the two cards. Fix is self-center on the wrapper, so the height comes from the ratio and the list cannot reach it; ratios are now aspect-[16/9] on mobile and sm:aspect-[5/7] from sm up, with object-cover object-center and overflow-hidden kept. Measured after: 160x224 in collapsed, expanded AND re-collapsed, identical, and both cards identical at ratio 0.714; 356x200 = 16/9 at 390px. w-full is kept alongside self-center deliberately because below sm the card is a flex column where align-self acts on the horizontal axis. Also changed the disclosure label from See All to See More (N more) as specified, added x-cloak to both label spans so they cannot both paint before Alpine boots, and gave the button an sr-only category name so seven See More buttons are not seven identical controls. (2) REMOVED THE BREADCRUMB from the single service page along with its mb-6, so the page opens directly on the grid with no empty band. It was hand-written markup, not a shared component - the only other mention of breadcrumb in resources/views is a comment in the customer appointments list - and the page still shows the service name as its h1 and the category as a chip, so nothing was lost. (3) IMPORTANT CORRECTION: the See More toggle was NOT broken. An earlier probe read the DOM synchronously after clicking and Alpine 3 batches its DOM writes in a microtask, so it saw stale values and looked dead. Re-tested with proper awaits: 4 -> 10 -> 4 rows, label swaps, aria-expanded false->true->false, scrollY stays 0, type=button present, zero console errors.
